Polling vs Long Polling

1. Polling (Short Polling)
Polling is the simplest approach to getting updates from a server. The client sends requests to the server at regular intervals, checking if anything has changed.

Think of it like refreshing your email inbox every few minutes to check for new messages.

How It Works:
a) Client sends an HTTP request to the server
b) Server responds immediately with current data (or empty response)
c) Client waits for a fixed interval (e.g., 5 seconds)
d) Client sends another request
e) Repeat indefinitely

|----------------------------------------------|

Client                         Server
  │                              │
  │──── GET /api/updates ───────►│
  │◄──── {"updates": []} ────────│
  │                              │
  │       Wait 5 seconds         │
  │                              │
  │──── GET /api/updates ───────►│
  │◄──── {"updates": []} ────────│
  │                              │
  │       Wait 5 seconds         │
  │                              │
  │──── GET /api/updates ───────►│
  │◄── {"updates":[{"id":1,...}]}│
  │                              │
  │       Wait 5 seconds         │
  │                              │
  │──── GET /api/updates ───────►│
  │◄──── {"updates": []} ────────│

|----------------------------------------------|

Notice something wasteful here? The client keeps asking even when nothing has changed. Three out of four requests in this diagram returned empty responses.

In real applications, this ratio is often much worse. You might make 100 requests before getting a single meaningful update.

Short polling can be used for:
Weather data
Periodic reports
Batch job status


2. Long Polling
Long polling improves on regular polling by having the server hold the request open until new data is available (or a timeout occurs). 
Instead of the client repeatedly asking “anything new?”, the server waits and responds only when there’s something to report.

This was the technique that powered early real-time applications like Facebook Messenger before WebSockets became widely supported.

How It Works:
a) Client sends an HTTP request to the server
b) Server holds the connection open (doesn’t respond immediately)
c) When new data arrives, server sends the response
d) Client immediately sends another request
e) If no data arrives within the timeout period, server sends an empty response and client reconnects

|----------------------------------------------|

Client                         Server
  │                              │
  │──── GET /api/updates?since=12345 ────►│
  │                              │
  │                              │ Holding connection open...
  │                              │ Waiting for new data...
  │                              │ 15 seconds pass...
  │                              │ New data arrives...
  │◄──── {"updates": [{"id": 12346, "message": "Hello!"}]} ────│
  │                              │
  │──── GET /api/updates?since=12346 ────►│
  │                              │
  │                              │ Holding connection open...
  │                              │ Waiting for new data...
  │                              │ 30 seconds pass, timeout
  │◄──── {"updates": []} ────────│
  │                              │
  │──── GET /api/updates?since=12346 ────►│
  │                              │
  │                              │ Holding connection open...
  │                              │

|----------------------------------------------|

Long polling can be used for:
Legacy chat applications
Real-time notifications
Systems where WebSockets aren't available or practical
